{
"dur": 84.003,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 7.32,
"text": "If this sounds too easy, you're not the first to think so. In 1963, a young anthropologist had the same doubt.",
"end": 7.595
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.595,
"vo_start": 7.87,
"vo_end": 13.144,
"text": "His name was Richard Lee. He went to the Dobe area on the edge of the Kalahari, in Botswana.",
"end": 13.419
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 13.419,
"vo_start": 13.694,
"vo_end": 18.253,
"text": "He lived with the Ju/'hoansi, people who at the time still lived largely by hunting and gathering.",
"end": 18.528
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 18.528,
"vo_start": 18.803,
"vo_end": 27.083,
"text": "The textbook story said life like this meant working every daylight hour just to survive. Lee decided not to assume it. He counted it.",
"end": 27.358
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 27.358,
"vo_start": 27.633,
"vo_end": 31.553,
"text": "For weeks, he recorded who went out for food, for how long, and what they brought back.",
"end": 31.828
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 31.828,
"vo_start": 32.103,
"vo_end": 39.328,
"text": "The result surprised everyone. Finding food took adults about fifteen hours a week. Roughly two and a half days.",
"end": 39.603
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 39.603,
"vo_start": 39.878,
"vo_end": 43.398,
"text": "If you work a normal job, you pass fifteen hours by Tuesday afternoon.",
"end": 43.673
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 43.673,
"vo_start": 43.948,
"vo_end": 52.85,
"text": "To be fair, that number is only food. When Lee later added tool-making, cracking nuts, cooking and chores, the total rose to around forty hours a week.",
"end": 53.125
},
{
"i": 9,
"start": 53.125,
"vo_start": 53.4,
"vo_end": 63.44,
"text": "That's close to a modern full-time job. But the shape was completely different. No commute, no fixed shift, no boss, and the work happened with family and friends around you.",
"end": 63.715
},
{
"i": 10,
"start": 63.715,
"vo_start": 63.99,
"vo_end": 75.23,
"text": "Researchers later studied other foraging groups: the Hadza in Tanzania, the Ach\u00e9 in Paraguay, the Martu in Australia. The numbers vary a lot. Some groups spend far longer finding food.",
"end": 75.505
},
{
"i": 11,
"start": 75.505,
"vo_start": 75.78,
"vo_end": 83.053,
"text": "So the real lesson isn't a magic number. It's who owned the hours. They decided when to work, how hard, and when to stop.",
"end": 84.003
}
],
